course: dictionary
lesson: 11292
title: Roman numerals: XIII
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: XIII is a Roman numeral, and it means thirteen. Roman numerals use letters instead of digits: X means 10 and I means 1.
explain: Read XIII from left to right. X is 10, then the three I's add 1 each time, so 10 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 13.
explain: You will meet XIII in names of kings, queens and popes, like «Pope Gregory XIII». The number shows which ruler with that name he was. You will also see it in titles and on some clock faces.
fact: XIII means 13.
fact: X means 10 and I means 1, so XIII is 10 + 1 + 1 + 1.
fact: Roman numerals are usually written in capitals.
fact: «In 1582 Pope Gregory XIII brought in the Gregorian calendar» to fix the calendar, which had slipped about 10 days.
fact: «Thursday 4 October was followed by Friday 15 October, skipping 10 dates.»
exercise: What is XIII in ordinary numbers? | 10 | 31 | 3 | *13
exercise: In «Louis XIII», what does XIII tell us? | How many horses he had | His favourite colour | The year he was born | *Which king it is, the thirteenth with that name
exercise: How do you write 3 in Roman numerals? | X | V | L | *III
test: In «chapter XIII», what does XIII tell the reader? | The name of the author | The number of pages in the book | The page where the chapter starts | *The chapter number, which is thirteen
test: Which number is XIII? | 103 | 31 | 11 | *13
exam: Using «In 1582 Pope Gregory XIII brought in the Gregorian calendar», which year did the calendar change? | 1492 | 1066 | 1600 | *1582
